<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="chapter-factors-multiples-primes">
  <title>Factors and Multiples</title>
  <objectives xml:id="li-factors-multiples"><title>Learning Intentions</title><introduction><p>After completing this chapter, you should be able to:</p></introduction><ol>
      <li><p>Recognise factors, multiples, primes and composite numbers.</p></li>
      <li><p>Find the prime factorisation of a positive integer.</p></li>
      <li><p>Calculate the highest common factor and lowest common multiple.</p></li>
    </ol></objectives>
  <introduction><p>Factors and multiples reveal the multiplicative structure of integers. Prime factorisation allows systematic calculations.</p></introduction>

  <section xml:id="factors-intro">
    <title>Introduction</title>
    <introduction><p>A factor divides a whole number without a remainder; a multiple is obtained by multiplying it by an integer. A prime number greater than one has exactly two positive factors, 1 and itself.</p></introduction>
    <p>Prime factorisation reveals the structure of positive integers. The <term>highest common factor</term> (HCF) uses the prime factors shared by the numbers, each with the lower power; the <term>lowest common multiple</term> (LCM) uses every prime factor needed, each with the higher power.</p>
    <p>For example, <m>12=2^2\cdot3</m> and <m>18=2\cdot3^2</m>. Their HCF is <m>2\cdot3=6</m>; their LCM is <m>2^2\cdot3^2=36</m>. The two ideas answer different questions: what divides both, and what is divisible by both?</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="factors-multiples-primes-practice">
    <title>Worked Example and Practice</title>
    <example xml:id="example-factors-multiples-primes">
      <title>Worked example</title>
      <statement><p>Express 60 as a product of prime factors.</p></statement>
      <solution><p>60=2×2×3×5, or 2²×3×5.</p></solution>
    </example>
    <exercise xml:id="exercise-factors-multiples-primes">
      <statement><p>Find the HCF of 12 and 18.</p></statement>
      <answer><p>The common factors are 1, 2, 3 and 6, so the HCF is 6.</p></answer>
    </exercise>
  </section>
</chapter>
